---
# Template. Copy to edge-ips.sops.yaml, fill in, encrypt:
#
#   cp edge-ips.sops.yaml.example edge-ips.sops.yaml
#   $EDITOR edge-ips.sops.yaml
#   sops -e -i edge-ips.sops.yaml
#
# The public-ingress host's public and mesh addresses, consumed as ${PUBLIC_IP}/${MESH_IP} via
# postBuild.substituteFrom in infra/traefik-edge/ks.yaml — and nowhere else. Two other consumers
# read this Secret once and no longer do: the traefik-edge scrape job discovers the mesh address
# from the API server (a hostNetwork pod's status.podIP is the kubelet's --node-ip, which
# k0sctl.yaml.j2 sets to the mesh IP), and netpol-allow-from-ingress-edge spells the mesh CIDR
# literally.
#
# What is left cannot be discovered: `hostIP` in a pod spec takes no fieldRef, so the two
# addresses traefik-edge binds have to be written down. That is the whole remaining reason this
# file exists.
#
# These used to be pushed into the cluster out-of-band by ansible/roles/flux_bootstrap, because
# there was no way to commit an identifying value. SOPS removes that constraint, so they live
# here instead: one source of truth, reviewable in a diff, and Flux no longer depends on ansible
# having run. The tradeoff is that both are values you maintain rather than ones discovered from
# the mesh — they are stable per node, but if you ever re-create a node, update this file. The
# canonical record is ansible/nodes/<node>/host.sops.yml, whose node_mesh_ip roles/netbird
# writes back after each join; keep this file in step with it.
#
# A Secret rather than a ConfigMap: substituteFrom accepts either, and SOPS encrypting a
# ConfigMap's data would be conspicuous mislabelling of something that is, in fact, hidden.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: edge-ips
  namespace: flux-system
type: Opaque
stringData:
  PUBLIC_IP: 203.0.113.10
  MESH_IP: 100.80.0.1
